SportMeter makes it easier for athletes to share how they're doing. With short questions about fun, energy, tension and engagement, athletes get a simple way to give a signal. That way, what's going on in the group no longer depends on loose conversations, gut feeling or chance.

### Why we push forward here

Many athletes don't say how they're doing by themselves. Sometimes because they find it hard to speak up, sometimes because the moment isn't there, and sometimes because coaches simply can't see everything. That's how small signals stay under the radar for too long.

With SportMeter we make that easier. Athletes answer short questions at a fixed moment. Not a long form, but a quick check on how things are going. That creates more visibility on fun, motivation and what needs attention in the group, without athletes having to start a conversation themselves.

### How does it work?

For athletes, SportMeter works from three simple places: your check-ins, your calendar and your guidance. That way you know when something is expected of you, you can briefly share how you're doing, and you get feedback on the themes your team is working on.

What does this mean for me?

1. You see your check-ins in one place. Think short questions about trainings, matches, energy, recovery, fun or how you feel in the group.

2. You fill in a check-in when it's ready. That usually takes about 60 seconds. You pick a score, give a short answer or explain something if you want to.

3. In your calendar you see trainings, matches and the moments a check-in or form is ready.

4. In guidance you see your team's focus. You can add notes, keep something private or share feedback with your coach.

5. Want to talk about something? Give a signal to your coach, trainer or a trusted person.

### Together for Fun

The strength of SportMeter is in simplicity and repetition: athletes briefly share how they're doing, coaches see faster what's going on and the club follows up on signals better. That way well-being doesn't depend on loose conversations or gut feeling, but becomes a fixed part of guidance.

